WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:22.020
The 
world has an eerie fascination with serial

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.020
killers.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.810
Whether it's hearing tales of the men who
process their victims through meat grinders

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:33.460
in order to feed the remains to future victims,
or stories of strange fetishes and incomprehensible

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.780
desires to commit atrocities upon corpses,
people can't help but be intrigued.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.899
This dark side of the human psyche has been
featured on screens big and small for decades,

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:49.149
and there is something undeniably captivating
about true crime.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.719
It doesn't let you look away.

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:54.570
Today, we're used to the idea of criminal
profiling.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.450
It's the science of looking at a crime and
its victims in order to understand everything

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.850
we can about its perpetrators.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.039
It's easy to forget that modern Criminology
is a relatively new science, pioneered by

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:10.790
one man who joined the Federal Bureau of Investigation
in 1970.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.931
Although he first started out in SWAT and
later became a hostage negotiator, it was

00:01:14.931 --> 00:01:20.700
when John E Douglas finally ended up in the
classroom at Quantico that he found his life's

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.700
calling.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.850
He spent much of his career investigating
some of the most violent, dangerous criminals

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.950
who have ever stalked and preyed upon the
innocent.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:34.310
Douglas logged years of work interviewing
dangerous men, trying to uncoil just what

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.560
made them tick.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.970
You may not recognize his name, but you certainly
recognize the characters that have been based

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.670
on him, and the shows inspired by his life.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.229
His story has been the inspiration for Silence
of the Lambs, as well as the show Criminal

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:49.229
Minds.

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:53.170
A fictionalized version of him was portrayed
in Netflix's Mindhunter.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:57.740
Douglas really did sit down with some of the
most notorious serial killers and violent

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:03.409
offenders the country has ever seen, using
what he learned from them to build the groundwork

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:05.770
for the science of criminal profiling.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.229
Unsurprisingly, spending years in the midst
of psychotic killers is a health hazard.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:17.110
As you’ll find out in today’s Biographics,
Douglas was fortunate to escape with his life.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:23.110
TITLE: Into the head of the devil

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.250
Douglas wrote the book Mindhunter: Inside
the FBI's Elite Serial Crime Unit about his

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.450
experiences in the early days of behavioral
analysis.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.310
The Netflix series of nearly the same name
took liberties with the story, as his life

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.709
was shaped into the character of Holden Ford.

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:40.540
While there was a bit of creative license
taken with the details, there was a lot of

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.200
truth in the show, too — starting with his
motives for deciding to interview serial killers

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.200
in the first place.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.920
Now, it's important to note that the FBI already
had a Behavioral Sciences Unit before Douglas

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.130
came on the scene.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:57.520
It was established in 1971, and law enforcement
already knew how valuable psychology could

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.260
be in tracking down criminals.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.300
But here's the thing: it wasn't systemized.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.900
There was no manual, no set of classifications
and definitions, no framework to base assessments

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.900
on.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.739
When Douglas transferred there in 1977 to
begin teaching hostage negotiation and applied

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:16.819
criminal psychology, the department was still
new.

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:18.519
And so was Douglas.

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:20.050
On paper, he looked great.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.090
He was going into the classroom with a few
years of military service behind him and seven

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.080
years as a field agent under his belt, but
he was still only 32 years old.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.150
And he'd seen how classrooms worked at Quantico.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.670
Instructors needed to be at the top of their
game because sometimes, they were teaching

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:40.480
the details of a case to agents and officers
who had actually worked the case.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.940
And those agents didn't hesitate to challenge
an instructor if they said something even

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.340
slightly wrong.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.410
Douglas realized that the fastest way to learn
cases and criminals was to go right to the

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:51.410
source.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.730
He needed to build up some serious credibility,
so he started talking to some of the nation's

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.620
most infamous criminals.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.050
Douglas began setting up interviews with the
most prolific serial offenders of his time,

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:05.610
and during that era, there were plenty of
men available to him.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:10.830
The 1970s, 80s, and 90s would become known
as a golden age of serial killers, with more

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:16.440
than 80 percent of the 20th century’s serials
cropping up during those three decades.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.630
Criminologists now believe the serial phenomenon
had something to do with the coming-of-age

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.410
of men who had been raised in the aftermath
of World War II, an event that left broken

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.490
families and broken people to raise their
children.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.630
Ted Bundy, Charles Manson, John Wayne Gacy.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.050
Ed Kemper, Richard Speck, David Berkowitz.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.180
The list is a long one, and it keeps going
far past the headliners.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:43.240
For the public at large, each name was associated
with a terrorized community, or a series of

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:50.240
gruesome crimes; for John Douglas, each name
was another corrupted mind to probe and explore.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.860
Douglas found that what he was learning from
some of the most deviant minds the bureau

00:04:53.860 --> 00:05:00.030
had ever crossed paths with could be applied
to all violent crimes, not just murders.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.970
At first, he said he started conducting interviews
"for survival," but it wasn't long before

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:09.580
he realized the work would allow him to provide
some valuable insights into future investigations.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.460
Of course, not everyone agreed with him or
his methods.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.460
His biggest detractor?

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.460
The FBI.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.640
TITLE: Douglas vs. the FBI

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:26.440
Douglas’ exploration into the criminal mind
has been undeniably invaluable, but at the

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.630
time he was actively conducting interviews,
the powers-that-be at the FBI didn't see it

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.280
that way.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.280
At all.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.910
The FBI was concerned, among other things,
about bureaucracy and the chain of command.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.979
Douglas was the one out in the field, traveling
the country, interviewing serial killers and

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:45.680
getting to know the local officers who were
actually on the front lines of all these cases.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.820
When everyday officers needed help on open
investigations, many local offices started

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:55.460
calling him directly, instead of jumping through
the hoops of a more official FBI communication

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.460
channel.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.850
Douglas never wanted to turn anyone away for
bureaucratic reasons, because he knew that

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:04.210
red tape was likely to discourage agents he
could quickly advise.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:08.580
Rather than scold officers or agents for coming
straight to him, he’d help them develop

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.910
a profile or advise on what information should
be released to the media.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:17.870
Sometimes, Douglas felt that conflict very
acutely in some of the most unlikely places.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:22.199
One of the first big cases that the young
Behavioral Science Unit consulted on was the

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:27.630
case Netflix's Mindhunter presents on center
stage: the Atlanta child murders.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:32.840
The case was real, and the killing spree began
with the 1979 murders of 13-year-old Alfred

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.319
Evans and 14-year-old Edward Hope Smith.

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:40.360
Originally thought to be just another drug-related
killing, it wasn't until somewhere around

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.690
19 more children either disappeared or turned
up dead that law enforcement began to connect

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:50.770
them, finally seeing what the community had
been trying to tell them all along: there

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.480
was a killer on the loose.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.660
Douglas went to Atlanta in 1981, walking the
woods where bodies had been found and reviewing

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:58.660
files.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.710
He wasn't exactly welcomed with open arms,
though.

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:04.759
After sitting down for an FBI-sanctioned interview
with People magazine — during which he said

00:07:04.759 --> 00:07:08.690
he was certain they were looking for a killer
who was black — he suddenly found himself

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:12.970
working with a field office that was less
than thrilled with the effects they believed

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.870
his comments were going to have.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.430
The entire situation was a powder keg.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:22.419
The community had already organized an intitiative
called STOP, which they did hoping to convince

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:25.490
the Atlanta Police Department to view the
killings as connected.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:29.990
But an undercurrent of racism and accusations
that some of the local cops were card-carrying

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:35.050
members of the Ku Klux Klan overshadowed any
earnest efforts Douglas was trying to make.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.240
At the time, the black community was certain
the KKK was responsible, and the idea that

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.590
a white FBI agent sat down and publicly declared
the killer was black just didn't sit well

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:49.509
with many and gave the impression that law
enforcement wasn't entirely on the side of

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:51.250
impartial justice.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:52.740
Douglas's comments just looked bad.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.849
It was a sentiment that may have been somewhat
assuaged had the FBI agents in the Atlanta

00:07:56.849 --> 00:08:01.890
field office been introduced to the media,
especially Special Agent in Charge John Glover,

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.910
who was the first black agent to lead a field
office.

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:07.910
There were no introductions, and instead,
Douglas was told repeatedly that he was on

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.120
the verge of starting a race war.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:14.610
Douglas found himself fighting an uphill battle
to get anyone to listen to his ideas, including

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.560
applying his profile to the attendees at the
Frank Sinatra and Sammy Davis Jr. benefit

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.509
concert, which was organized to help fund
the city's massive manhunt.

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:27.080
He'd hoped to get agents on board with his
case theory on vetting all attendees at the

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.970
concert, which would have eliminated women
and suspects without a car before moving on

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:31.979
to racial filters.

00:08:31.979 --> 00:08:36.160
Douglas hoped that process would give them
a pool of suspects to investigate further,

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.390
but it was never done.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.150
Sadly, it might have helped.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:42.930
Wayne Williams, the man later arrested in
connection with at least some of the murders,

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:45.880
not only fit the profile, but was at the concert.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:50.570
Douglas long maintained Williams was the right
collar for some of the cases, but he's also

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:54.010
remained steadfast in his belief that he wasn't
the only killer.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:58.300
That was based on not just his assessment
of the crimes, but of patterns seen in the

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.300
choice of victims.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:03.630
As for Williams, he was convicted of the murder
of two adults associated with the case, but

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.420
the murders of the children have remained
unsolved.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:47.779
The case was reopened

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:52.460
in August of 2019.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:59.370
TITLE: The perfect interview

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:24.390
What is

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:29.740
the one thing that anyone fascinated by true
crime and psychology would love to do a deep

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.140
dive into?

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:36.380
The transcripts and recordings of Douglas'
interviews with people like Charles Manson

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:37.380
and Ed Kemper, right?

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.940
Here's the thing: transcripts and tapes of
most of his interviews don't exist.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.760
Part of Douglas’ work wasn't just in figuring
out the right questions to ask -- it was figuring

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:51.880
out how to get them to answer and how to interpret
those answers.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.490
When he first began conducting his interviews,
he brought along a tape recorder, but it wasn't

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.400
long before he realized that the recording
was a huge mistake that was only making his

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.290
subjects suspicious of him.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:05.700
Douglas was already at a disadvantage going
in, as a member of the law enforcement system

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:09.140
that had arrested, tried, convicted, and sentenced
them.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:13.050
He already represented the authority that
ordered them around every day, and when he

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:17.190
recorded conversations or tried to write them
down, he found that two things happened.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:21.820
His subjects grew suspicious that what they
were saying was going to be used against them

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:24.410
somehow, and when he wrote, they grew angry
and belligerent.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:25.410
They wanted his full attention, and they weren't
getting it if he was looking elsewhere.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.440
In order to get them to talk, Douglas knew
he had to create an environment that would

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.510
facilitate a dialogue, and that meant he had
to skip a recording device.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.740
He had to rely on memorization.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:39.240
There are a few things he discovered were
necessary to getting the information he wanted,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.520
and it started before he even set foot in
a prison.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:46.300
Preparation was essential, and he would spend
time going through every aspect of the cases

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.720
associated with the person he was speaking
to.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.329
Police reports, crime scene photos, autopsy
details… it was a grisly amount of information

00:11:53.329 --> 00:11:57.640
to sift through, but it would allow him to
know when the offender was being truthful

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.149
and when he was lying.

00:11:59.149 --> 00:12:03.200
And they lied a lot; if he could call them
out on it, though, that went a long way in

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.610
establishing a relationship.

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:09.149
It allowed him to demonstrate how well he
knew their case or how closely he'd studied

00:12:09.149 --> 00:12:10.149
them.

00:12:10.149 --> 00:12:11.760
To a serial killer, that's flattering.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:16.300
After the memorization game, most everything
else came down to comfort and control, but

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.430
he learned that control is a tricky thing.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:23.000
There's a scene that happens at least once
an episode in almost every police procedural:

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.720
law enforcement goes into a room where a suspect
is being held and confronts them.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:31.190
There's a lot of anger, some posturing, a
lot of shouting, and usually a lot of accusations.

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:35.160
That is the exact opposite of the atmosphere
Douglas knew he needed to create.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:40.110
He always stressed that it wasn't an interrogation,
it was a conversation.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:43.790
Conversations go both ways, and often times,
that meant killers had questions for him.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:47.560
They asked about his job, his family… and
he answered, truthfully.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.340
By answering the questions asked of him, he
could create the illusion that they were the

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.460
ones in control.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:55.209
They knew something about him, and it made
them giddy.

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:58.519
Over the years, Douglas built up a playbook
for interview techniques.

00:12:58.519 --> 00:13:02.670
He outlined how important the setup of the
room was, and stressed that seating arrangements

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.589
should be made based on personality.

00:13:04.589 --> 00:13:08.740
For example, he gave paranoid individuals
the chance to be seated so they could always

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:14.010
see a door or a window, and dominant personalities
like Charles Manson would be given the chance

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.300
to sit above him, looking down.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.040
And they usually took it.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.870
Certain words, like "murder" and "killing"
were never used.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:24.410
Blame was never projected, and disgust or
judgement was never shown.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:28.970
He was always careful to keep his body language
and posture relaxed and neutral, and even

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:32.240
likened it to the sort of way one might act
on a first date.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:36.500
There was never any negativity, but feigning
empathy was sometimes a must.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:38.450
Sympathy for the devil, indeed.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:43.080
In the end, it was often one simple thing
that got many of recent history's most notorious

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:47.470
serial killers to open up to him: he played
to their narcissism and their sense of pride

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.910
in themselves and in their crimes.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:51.910
In short?

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.722
Given the chance, many killers love to talk
about themselves, especially if they have

00:13:54.722 --> 00:13:58.610
a captive audience.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:02.143
TITLE: The worst of the worst

00:14:02.143 --> 00:14:06.550
For many ordinary folk, the idea of coming
face-to-face with a serial killer is terrifying.

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:11.170
But who did someone who interviewed serial
killers on a regular basis rank as the worst

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:12.170
of the worst?

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:13.899
For starters, Lawrence Bittaker.

00:14:13.899 --> 00:14:17.769
In 1978, Bittaker met Roy Norris at the California
State Prison.

00:14:17.769 --> 00:14:22.230
They shared a morbid fantasy of kidnapping
and killing teenage girls after brutally torturing

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:23.230
them.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:27.079
And they did, killing five girls between the
ages of 13 and 18.

00:14:27.079 --> 00:14:30.519
Douglas thought he was particularly bad because
he looked so ordinary.

00:14:30.519 --> 00:14:31.519
Normal.

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:35.740
The sort of guy these teenage hitchhikers
wouldn't have thought twice about getting

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:36.740
in a van with.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.520
Unfortunately for them, he had nicknamed his
van the "Murder Mack".

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:45.410
Bittaker and Norris made auto recordings as
they tortured the girls, and Douglas had the

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:46.410
tapes.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.399
When Scott Glen was getting ready to play
his fictional counterpart in Silence of the

00:14:49.399 --> 00:14:51.839
Lambs, he had the actor come to his office.

00:14:51.839 --> 00:14:56.160
He played the tapes, and says Glen's response
was emotionally charged.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.949
"John," he said, "I was against the death
penalty, but I didn't know there are people

00:15:00.949 --> 00:15:02.050
like that."

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:05.839
The interview that stood out to Douglas as
one of the most fascinating also has a Silence

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:10.649
of the Lambs connection, and it was the killer
who was the inspiration for Buffalo Bill.

00:15:10.649 --> 00:15:15.230
Gary Heidnik was convicted in 1989, found
guilty of holding women in what came to be

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:20.290
known as Heidnik's House of Horrors as he
beat, raped, tortured, and killed them, forcing

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:24.959
his surviving captives to help dismember the
dead, then mixing the remains with dog food

00:15:24.959 --> 00:15:26.720
and feeding it to his prisoners.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.260
Douglas has also talked about the one truly
unnerving thing that all interviews have in

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:33.290
common: the environment.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.529
He said: "...going into prisons, the doors
clanging behind you.

00:15:36.529 --> 00:15:39.089
The noise level in the prisons, it's just
so loud.

00:15:39.089 --> 00:15:40.660
I don't trust the inmates.

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.389
At times, I don't even trust corrections…"

00:15:44.389 --> 00:15:49.930
TITLE: The warning signs

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:53.600
As Douglas collected more information, he
and his colleagues began compiling it into

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:59.029
a centralized database called ViCAP, or the
Violent Criminal Apprehension Program, and

00:15:59.029 --> 00:16:02.470
it's worth a quick aside to mention two of
those colleagues.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:06.329
Douglas wasn't working alone; he had a partner
in FBI agent Robert Ressler.

00:16:06.329 --> 00:16:10.250
Ressler joined the FBI in 1970 and transferred
to the BSU when it was formed.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.130
He's the one who coined the term "serial killer".

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:17.310
Douglas also worked with a forensic nurse
named Ann Wolbert Burgess, who started out

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:21.490
working with the victims of sexual abuse and
did some of the first formalized studies of

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.009
rape cases in Boston.

00:16:23.009 --> 00:16:27.440
The FBI director at the time, William Webster,
saw it as valuable information his agents

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.709
needed to be trained in, and later, Burgess
was brought on board to help translate what

00:16:31.709 --> 00:16:36.449
Douglas and Ressler were learning into academic
terms that could be applied to other cases.

00:16:36.449 --> 00:16:41.130
Together with a small team who Burgess remembers
as not getting much resistance from the powers-that-be,

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:45.420
simply because "it wasn't seen as important
at the time," Douglas began to try to make

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:49.760
sense of what they were learning from the
criminals they were interviewing, and patterns

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:50.840
began to emerge.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:55.730
The first was the idea that a killer could
be either organized or disorganized, essentially

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:58.769
meaning they either planned their crimes or
did not.

00:16:58.769 --> 00:17:02.759
From there, they developed a method of looking
for patterns within the crimes, signatures

00:17:02.759 --> 00:17:07.120
specific to certain killers that could be
used to tie murders together, and behaviors

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:08.510
that hinted at motivation.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:13.200
As he has often said, "If you want to understand
the artist, look at his art."

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:19.410
Douglas determined that at the heart of serial
criminals of almost any kind are three words:

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:20.450
"Manipulation.

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:21.490
Domination.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:22.490
Control."

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:23.870
Why are these driving factors there?

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:27.960
According to Douglas, his interviews uncovered
a pattern now familiar to anyone who watches

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:28.980
crime shows.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:33.490
The behavior of predators was formed in childhood,
when, he said, they "all grew up without forming

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:37.800
trusting bonds with other human beings during
their formative years."

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:41.980
He developed ideas regarding the questions
of nature vs. nurture, too.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.750
Are serial killers born or made?

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:50.160
He argued: "...while no one who does not have
certain inborn tendencies toward impulsivity,

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:55.690
anger, and/or sadistic perversion is going
to evolve into a predator because of bad upbringing,

00:17:55.690 --> 00:18:00.590
there is no doubt in my mind that those possessing
such inborn tendencies can be pushed along

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:06.190
the path to predation by negative influences
as they grow up and mature."

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:10.280
Many of these ideas are embodied in one of
the most notorious serial killers that Douglas

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:11.280
ever interviewed.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.260
When Douglas talked to Ed Kemper, he was in
jail for killing his mother, her friend, and

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:16.549
six college students.

00:18:16.549 --> 00:18:21.320
Kemper gave a full confession and showed police
where to find the bodies, despite the fact

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:23.559
that he was well-known to those police.

00:18:23.559 --> 00:18:27.650
Some called him "Big Ed," and he liked to
hang out and drink with them at a local bar.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:31.350
As Douglas talked to him, he found the roots
of his crimes went back into childhood.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:35.640
He had a terrible relationship with his abusive
mother, and after being relentlessly bullied

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:39.660
and abused by his grandparents, he killed
them both when he was just 15.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:44.429
His history of violence went back even further,
to when a 10-year-old Kemper killed the family's

00:18:44.429 --> 00:18:45.429
cat.

00:18:45.429 --> 00:18:49.340
Kemper embodied something Douglas found in
all serial predators he interviewed, and he

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:54.200
described it as an internal conflict where
feelings of self-importance, grandiosity,

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:58.810
and superiority were constantly at war with
feelings of inadequacy and inferiority.

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:03.370
Add in a childhood full of abuse, and it's
a recipe for a killer.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:05.910
And much of the time, the signs are there
from childhood.

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.990
Douglas described another pattern he saw,
called "the homicidal triad".

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:14.679
It's cruelty to animals, starting fires, and
bed-wetting, a group of behaviors that can

00:19:14.679 --> 00:19:16.990
indicate something is deeply wrong.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:21.770
None of it, he was careful to stress, excused
any of their actions as in the end.

00:19:21.770 --> 00:19:25.429
They did what they did of their own free will.

00:19:25.429 --> 00:19:31.480
TITLE: Stare long enough into the darkness...

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:36.010
Douglas spent almost his entire career getting
inside the heads of serial offenders who have

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:41.490
acted on some of their deepest, darkest desires,
fantasies so horrible they would have made

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:42.650
the devil blush.

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:45.490
Did they get into his head in return?

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:46.490
Absolutely.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:50.630
In 1983, he headed to Seattle to consult on
the Green River Murder case.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:54.400
He said goodbye to his wife at their home,
then, on a hunch, he headed to the school

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:56.810
where she was teaching and said goodbye again.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:20:01.040
By the time he hit Seattle he had a raging
headache, and told the other agents he was

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:03.370
working with that he thought he was getting
the flu.

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:07.530
That was Tuesday night, and he told them that
he was taking off until Friday.

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:11.190
The week went by, Friday came along, and no
one had heard from him.

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:14.470
They kicked open his door — Do Not Disturb
sign and all — and found him.

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:17.570
He had collapsed, and spent three days on
the floor in a coma.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:22.230
The official diagnosis was viral encephalitis,
and post-traumatic stress disorder.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:27.190
He spent five months in rehab, and was told
that something needed to change.

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:28.190
Did it?

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:29.200
That's debatable.

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:33.320
Douglas oversaw an average of 1,000 cases
each and every year, up until his retirement

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:34.600
in 1995.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:39.070
Post-retirement, he continued to write about
the cases he studied, give lectures, and work

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:44.049
on high-profile cases like the JonBenet Ramsey
case and the West Memphis Three.

00:20:44.049 --> 00:20:46.050
The nightmares were worse when he was young.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:50.590
Part of that might be because of his belief
that the night is the best time for quiet

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:55.280
introspection, for going over the details
of cases, crime scenes, and interviews, of

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.570
dissecting everything for every last bit of
information.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:03.230
It's hard to turn off what he saw and heard
during the day, and while it's a vital part

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:08.370
of being in law enforcement to become desensitized
to certain sights and sounds, Douglas couldn't

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:09.740
allow himself to do that.

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:14.150
Not when he was trying to learn what made
them do the things they had done.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.150
"What I do…

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:18.740
I felt like I could not put up any kind of
wall,” Douglas said.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:23.860
“To really understand and interpret a crime,
you have to walk in the shoes of the offender

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:25.830
as well as the victim…"

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:46.690
And that means visualizing everything.

